public/
resources/
.hugo_build.lock
# ⛔ BROWSER-PROOF SCREENSHOTS ARE NOT WRITTEN HERE ANY MORE, and this is the
# second line of defence rather than the rule.
#
# Every proof used to default to "." — the working tree — and eleven of the
# resulting PNGs were TRACKED. One `go test ./...` dirtied nine of them, so
# `git status` stopped being a usable signal, `gh pr create` warned about
# uncommitted changes on branches that had not touched them (which is how a
# screenshot gets committed into a dependency bump by accident), and each commit
# carrying one added a few hundred kilobytes to a history that never gives them
# back. The list below used to name eight of them, at the ROOT, from runs in a
# different directory — a list that only ever grew.
#
# They now go to the user's own application-data directory, chosen by
# playground/internal/proofdir, which REFUSES any directory inside a repository.
# The rule lives there because a .gitignore is a safety net and not a barrier:
# `git add -f`, a fresh clone, or any tool that does not consult it commits the
# file anyway.
*-proof.png
*-proof-A.png
*-proof-B.png
playground/git-panel.png

# The playground wasm is a BUILD ARTIFACT: pages.yml builds it into static/
# before hugo runs. It was committed once, went 35 commits stale, and a CDN
# fallback served that stale copy to visitors. Never commit it again.
static/*.wasm

# `go build ./cmd/playground-wasm` with no -o drops a ~21 MB NATIVE binary here,
# named after the package directory. It was committed that way once (c7dbdd2) and
# every later build silently re-committed it. It is a build artifact; the wasm the
# site serves is built by pages.yml.
playground/playground-wasm
playground/git-worker
# The same binary lands in the COMMAND'S OWN directory when the build is run
# from inside it (`cd playground/cmd/git-worker && go build ./...`), which the
# two paths above do not cover — an 18 MB git-worker reached the index that way.
playground/cmd/playground-wasm/playground-wasm
playground/cmd/git-worker/git-worker
